    "discipline": "Presented ranked by evidential weight, not side by side as equals. The three unexcluded rivals remain in the confidence discussion and are not treated as excluded merely because no evidence currently supports them."
  },

  "lenses": [
    {
      "name": "Epistemology",
      "coreInsight": "'First come, first served' passes as fair because 'being free at the same moment' is silently treated as a starting position everyone holds. The moment that assumption is written down, the rule's neutrality is gone.",
      "keyFramework": "Assumption of identical starting points (that everyone commands the same discretionary time)",
      "limitation": "Cannot size the asymmetry.",
      "tension": "Consistent with the economics lens; no substantive conflict."
    },
    {
      "name": "Systems",
      "coreInsight": "No-shows are not a deviation but a stable output: certainty of holding plus zero cost of not releasing forms a positive feedback loop. Demand that is locked out never reports back into the system, because the locked-out have no trace to leave.",
      "keyFramework": "Feedback loop with the negative-feedback channel missing",
      "limitation": "Loop strength cannot be measured; only its direction can be stated.",
      "tension": "No conflict with institutional analysis; it supplies the half of the answer about why the problem never corrects itself."
    },
    {
      "name": "Institutional analysis",
      "coreInsight": "The rule is written by the library, its cost is borne by students in class, and the decision-maker is not the cost-bearer — the classic shape of a misaligned rule that persists. The two administrative lines (timetabling and booking) have no interface at all, and the cost falls exactly in that seam.",
      "keyFramework": "Written rule versus actual result (formal compliance concealing substantive consequence)",
      "limitation": "Cannot establish whether management has the will or the budget to reform.",
      "tension": "Consistent with the political-science lens: nobody benefits outright, and nobody holds authority to end it."
    },
    {
      "name": "Temporality",
      "coreInsight": "What is scarce here is not seats but free time. The seat merely converts a difference in time allocation into a countable form: whoever's hours are institutionally claimed is permanently disadvantaged under this rule, irrespective of effort.",
      "keyFramework": "Time compression (the end whose discretionary time is compressed systematically loses)",
      "limitation": "The hours claimed by the timetable are not quantified.",
      "tension": "Complementary to the economics lens: one says the incentive is misaligned, the other says its carrier is time."
    },
    {
      "name": "Psychology",
      "coreInsight": "Having booked produces a sense of quasi-ownership, so holders will not release a seat even when they will not use it — loss aversion rather than malice. Repeatedly failing to book produces learned withdrawal: students stop trying, and their demand then vanishes from the counts entirely. That is silenced evidence, not absence of need.",
      "keyFramework": "Loss aversion plus learned helplessness (silenced evidence)",
      "limitation": "A mechanism hypothesis with no scale or observation behind it.",
      "tension": "Mutually reinforcing with the systems lens: demand disappearing is why the system never faces pressure to correct itself."
    },
    {
      "name": "Game completeness",
      "coreInsight": "Only students and the booking system are at the table, but the variables that decide the outcome are held by two parties who are not: timetabling (which sets the class hours) and library management (which sets the rule and bears the reform risk). Teaching staff are absent from the table yet in practice fix which hours are unavailable to Group B. A two-party game is in fact a game of four or more, and the absentees hold the levers.",
      "keyFramework": "Absent stakeholders and external variable-holders (off-table but outcome-deciding)",
      "limitation": "No party's actual position is known; this is structural inference.",
      "tension": "Consistent with institutional analysis but makes explicit the question of who can change the rule."
    }
  ],
  "lensDiscipline": {
    "comfortZoneEscapes": "Economics and institutional analysis are the comfortable register for an allocation question; two lenses sit outside it — temporality (foundational register) and game completeness (structural register).",
    "historyLens": "Not selected: the case is a constructed teaching situation with no historical lineage to draw precedents from. Recorded explicitly as an exemption rather than passed over in silence.",
    "eachLensBoundToFact": "Every lens insight is tied to a fact supplied by the case (100 seats, first-come rule, A/B timetable difference, no extra capacity, no individual identification)."
  },

  "tools": [
    {
      "name": "Incentive-structure mapping",
      "applied": "For each party, ask who decides, who pays and who gains, then check whether the gain window and the cost window line up in time.",
      "finding": "Decision-makers and cost-bearers are systematically separated, and gains are immediate while costs are delayed and externalized. This explains why a misaligned rule is emergent rather than designed by anyone."
